// The `interview` format, v0. This file is NORMATIVE: the Rust parser in
// diet/src/formats/interview.rs implements it and is not itself the
// definition.
//
// An interview answer is the prose a forked model returns when asked what it
// decided, learned, or got stuck on this turn. It was the most-parsed format
// in the system and the largest single source of SILENT LOSS: five defects in
// one lane, every one of them content discarded or corrupted with no signal.
//
//   * `**DECISION**:` captured the bold markers as part of the value, so a
//     legitimate entry was recorded as noise.
//   * A wrapper code fence around the whole answer made the parser see zero
//     fields.
//   * A tag-stripping step removed a leading `#` that was part of the content.
//   * A field's second and later lines were dropped: 71 of 630 archived
//     answers lost content that way.
//   * A close-cousin register (`Decision —` for `DECISION:`) matched nothing.
//
// Each was fixed by making the parser more tolerant, and every tolerance fix
// shipped its own regression. The lesson that closed the class: TOLERANCE IS
// NOT THE FIX, A GRAMMAR IS. What the register may be is stated here, as data,
// once -- and every shape ever observed is a fixture, so the register cannot
// drift silently again.
//
// Two rules deserve their reasons stated, because both look like tolerance and
// are not:
//
//   * THE TAG VOCABULARY IS CLOSED. A line is a tag line only if it opens with
//     a name this grammar lists. The alternative -- treat any `Word:` as a tag
//     -- reintroduces the continuation bug in a new dress, because ordinary
//     prose contains colons (`The bug: the parser dropped lines`) and every
//     one of them would end the field it sits in. Text that names no known
//     field is kept as an unparseable segment, raw. Never guessed at, never
//     dropped.
//   * A CONTINUATION IS ANY LINE THAT IS NOT THE NEXT TAG. Not "an indented
//     line", not "a line before a blank line": those heuristics are what lost
//     the 71. A field ends where the next field begins, and nowhere else.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// document
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Three shapes, in order. An emitter told to answer in a code block wraps the
// whole answer in one; an emitter that hit its token cap mid-block opened a
// fence it never closed. The third alternative is an answer with no wrapper.
//
// `unterminated` exists so that truncation is a shape in the grammar rather
// than a guess in the parser: an answer cut off mid-emission is a typed
// outcome, never "malformed".
document = { SOI ~ (wrapped | unterminated | bare) ~ EOI }

// An answer that opened with a fence: the LAST fence line in the document is
// that wrapper's closing one, and everything between is the answer.
wrapped = { fence_line ~ fenced_body ~ final_fence }

// The same, cut off before the wrapper closed.
unterminated = { fence_line ~ plain_body }

// No wrapper. Nothing here is a wrapper's closing fence, so a fence on the
// last line belongs to the value it sits in.
bare = { plain_body }

// The two bodies differ in ONE guard, and the duplication is deliberate. A
// wrapped body must refuse to read the final fence as content, or the wrapper
// swallows it; an unwrapped body must NOT refuse, or an answer whose value
// ends in a code block silently loses its last line -- which is the same
// silent-loss defect this format exists to close, arriving from the other
// direction.
fenced_body = { fenced_preamble? ~ fenced_field* }
fenced_preamble = { fenced_loose+ }
fenced_field = { tag_line ~ fenced_loose* }
fenced_loose = { !tag_line ~ !final_fence ~ text_line }

plain_body = { plain_preamble? ~ plain_field* }
plain_preamble = { plain_loose+ }
plain_field = { tag_line ~ plain_loose* }
plain_loose = { !tag_line ~ text_line }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// tag lines
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The registers, all of them, in one place:
//
//   DECISION: value              bare
//   **DECISION**: value          emphasised, any of the markdown emphases
//   ## DECISION                  heading, value on the lines below
//   ## DECISION: value           heading and separator together
//   Decision — value             cousin register, any of the separators
//   decision: value              any case
//
// The emphasis markers are matched as a PAIR (`PUSH`/`POP`), so `**DECISION*`
// is not a tag line and `**DECISION**` never leaves an asterisk in the value.
// That is defect one, stated as a rule.
tag_line = { hspace* ~ (heading_tag | inline_tag) ~ hspace* ~ value_head ~ line_end }

heading_tag = { heading_marker ~ hspace* ~ tag_body ~ (hspace* ~ separator)? }
inline_tag = { tag_body ~ hspace* ~ separator }

tag_body = { emphasised_tag | tag_name }
emphasised_tag = { PUSH(emphasis) ~ tag_name ~ POP }
emphasis = { "***" | "**" | "__" | "*" | "_" | "`" }

// `#` opens a heading only in front of a tag name. A `#` at the head of a
// value line is content and stays there -- defect three, stated as a rule.
heading_marker = { "#"+ }

separator = { ":" | "：" | "—" | "–" | "--" | "-" | "=" | "»" | ">" }

// The closed vocabulary. Longest alternative first where one is a prefix of
// another, because PEG choice is ordered.
tag_name = {
    ^"api_surface" | ^"api surface" | ^"api-surface"
  | ^"decision" | ^"evidence" | ^"learned" | ^"stuck" | ^"plan"
}

// The remainder of the tag line: the field's first line of value, which may
// be empty when the value starts on the line below.
value_head = @{ (!nl ~ ANY)* }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// lines, fences, whitespace
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Consumes a newline, or at least one character at end of input. A rule that
// can match the empty string inside a repetition never terminates.
text_line = { line_content ~ nl | line_rest }
line_content = @{ (!nl ~ ANY)* }
line_rest = @{ (!nl ~ ANY)+ ~ &EOI }

// A fence line, and the fence that closes a wrapper: the last one in the
// document. A fence inside a value is an ordinary line and stays in the value.
fence_line = { hspace* ~ fence_marker ~ (!nl ~ ANY)* ~ line_end }
final_fence = { fence_line ~ trailing* ~ &EOI }
fence_marker = { "```" | "~~~" }

trailing = _{ hspace | nl }

line_end = _{ nl | &EOI }
nl = _{ "\r\n" | "\n" }
hspace = _{ " " | "\t" }
